 ruthless ambition profoundly shaped the trajectory of Joseon court politics during the 15th century, establishing precedents for ministerial dominance and the intricate web of familial and factional alliances that would characterize later periods.

Myŏnghoe's ascent began in 1453 when he played a crucial role in the Gyeyu Jeongnan, a coup orchestrated by Prince Suyang (later King Sejo) to depose the young King Danjong. As a trusted confidant and strategist to Suyang, Han Myŏnghoe helped plan and execute the coup, subsequently being rewarded with high office and immense power. He served as Chief State Councillor twice and held various other ministerial positions, consolidating his influence through strategic marriages, including those of his daughters to King Yejong and King Seongjong, effectively making him the father-in-law to two successive monarchs. His political machinations ensured his family's prominence and his own enduring grip on the levers of state.

The legacy of Han Myŏnghoe is complex and contentious. While lauded by some for his administrative capabilities and contributions to consolidating Joseon's early bureaucratic framework, he is also remembered as a symbol of ministerial overreach and the dangers of unchecked political power. His actions contributed to a period of significant political instability and the rise of powerful factions within the court, paving the way for the literati purges (sahwa) that would plague later Joseon reigns. His posthumous disgrace and subsequent rehabilitation reflect the shifting historical interpretations of his role in shaping the dynasty."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T06:15:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32222467128", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1415-11-26, Seoul"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "1415-11-26, Seoul"}], "newValue": ["The political instability following the premature death of King Munjong and the minority of King Danjong created a power vacuum within the Joseon court.", "Han Myŏnghoe's strategic alliance with Prince Suyang provided him with an opportunity to ascend through active participation in a coup.", "The Joseon Dynasty's meritocratic civil service system, albeit manipulated, allowed individuals like Han Myŏnghoe to rise to prominence based on perceived abilities and loyalty."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T06:15:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32222467128", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "1487-11-28"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1487-11-28"}], "newValue": ["Han Myŏnghoe's actions significantl